<+># ComponentDefinition-backed Config Design\n\n## Summary\n\nThis design uses the existing `ComponentDefinition` API as the registry for\nconfig templates. A new `Config` custom resource references one restricted\n`ComponentDefinition` and supplies properties. The Config controller renders\nthe definition into a primary Kubernetes Secret and optional auxiliary\nresources.\n\nThe design removes the need for a separate `ConfigTemplate` CRD and\nConfigTemplate controller. It does not remove the Config controller: a\ncontroller must still watch Config resources, render their definitions, apply\nthe resulting objects, report status, and clean resources up.\n\n```text\nComponentDefinition (marked as a config template)\n                         |\n                         | referenced by\n                         v\n                     Config CR\n                         |\n                         | rendered by Config controller\n                         v\n            Primary Secret + optional outputs\n```\n\n## Background\n\nKubeVela's existing config subsystem is a templating layer for reusable\nconfiguration. A platform team publishes a CUE config template, and application\nteams instantiate it with different properties. The primary rendered object is\na Secret. A template may also render arbitrary Kubernetes resources through its\n`outputs` block.\n\nThe existing contract includes more than CUE evaluation:\n\n- `template.output` must produce the primary Secret.\n- `template.outputs` may produce auxiliary Kubernetes objects.\n- KubeVela adds protected labels and annotations to the Secret.\n- The original input properties are stored in the Secret for non-sensitive\n  read and list operations.\n- Sensitive configs cannot be returned by config read paths.\n- Auxiliary object references are tracked for deletion.\n- Expanded writers may send rendered configuration to an external system.\n- Distribution uses an Application and topology policies to deliver the Secret\n  to other clusters or namespaces.\n\nThe proposed declarative API must either preserve these behaviors or explicitly\nidentify them as compatibility gaps.\n\n## Why ComponentDefinition fits\n\n`ComponentDefinition` already provides the capabilities needed to register a\nreusable CUE template:\n\n- CUE parameter schema generation\n- definition validation\n- definition revisions\n- `output` and `outputs` rendering\n- namespaced and system-level definition lookup\n- existing CLI and documentation tooling\n\nCreating another template API duplicates much of this machinery. A dedicated\nConfigTemplate controller currently compiles the CUE template, extracts its\nparameter schema, and writes readiness information. The existing\nComponentDefinition controller already performs the corresponding definition\nregistration, schema, and revision work.\n\nThe two concepts are not completely interchangeable. A normal component is\nrendered as part of an Application and may use traits, policies, workflow data,\nand the full Application context. A Config has a smaller lifecycle and a stable\nSecret-oriented contract. For that reason, Config should reuse the definition\nformat and rendering engine without pretending to be a complete Application.\n\n## Decision\n\nUse labeled `ComponentDefinition` resources as config templates and retain one\nfocused Config controller.\n\nA ComponentDefinition is eligible for use by Config only when it has:\n\n```yaml\nmetadata:\n  labels:\n    config.oam.dev/template: \"true\"\n```\n\nThe Config controller enforces these additional restrictions:\n\n1. The definition's declared workload kind must be `Secret` in API version\n   `v1`.\n2. The primary `output` must render a valid Secret.\n3. `outputs` may contain arbitrary Kubernetes resources.\n4. Traits, policies, dependencies, workflow inputs, and workflow outputs are\n   not part of the Config API.\n5. Only `context.name` and `context.namespace` are guaranteed to templates.\n\nThis boundary retains the existing meaning of a KubeVela config while reusing\nthe ComponentDefinition infrastructure.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n### Config backed by ComponentDefinition\n\nThe Config controller loads and renders a marked ComponentDefinition directly.\nThis reuses definition registration while keeping config-specific lifecycle and\nsecurity behavior in one place.\n\nThis is the selected approach.\n\n### Config creates a child Application\n\nThe Config controller could translate every Config into a one-component\nApplication. This would reuse the complete Application rendering and apply\npipeline, but it would introduce ApplicationRevisions and ResourceTrackers for\neach Config. It would also make sensitive properties easier to expose through\nApplication specs, revisions, or status and would complicate config status and\ndistribution.\n\n### Use Application directly\n\nUsers could instantiate a config ComponentDefinition in an Application without\nintroducing a Config CRD. This requires no Config controller, but loses the\ndedicated config API, config CLI behavior, sensitive reads, config listing,\nSecret metadata contract, and independent config lifecycle.\n\n## API design\n\n### Config template definition\n\nThe following definition renders an image registry Secret and an optional\nConfigMap containing non-sensitive registry metadata.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: core.oam.dev/v1beta1\nkind: ComponentDefinition\nmetadata:\n  name: image-registry-config\n  namespace: vela-system\n  labels:\n    config.oam.dev/template: \"true\"\n  annotations:\n    config.oam.dev/alias: Image Registry\n    config.oam.dev/description: Docker registry authentication\n    config.oam.dev/scope: namespace\n    config.oam.dev/sensitive: \"true\"\nspec:\n  workload:\n    definition:\n      apiVersion: v1\n      kind: Secret\n  schematic:\n    cue:\n      template: |\n        import (\n          \"encoding/base64\"\n          \"encoding/json\"\n        )\n\n        output: {\n          apiVersion: \"v1\"\n          kind:       \"Secret\"\n          metadata: {\n            name:      context.name\n            namespace: context.namespace\n          }\n          type: \"kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson\"\n          stringData: {\n            \".dockerconfigjson\": json.Marshal({\n              auths: {\n                \"\\(parameter.registry)\": {\n                  username: parameter.username\n                  password: parameter.password\n                  auth: base64.Encode(\n                    null,\n                    parameter.username + \":\" + parameter.password,\n                  )\n                }\n              }\n            })\n          }\n        }\n\n        outputs: registryInfo: {\n          apiVersion: \"v1\"\n          kind:       \"ConfigMap\"\n          metadata: {\n            name:      context.name + \"-info\"\n            namespace: context.namespace\n          }\n          data: {\n            registry: parameter.registry\n          }\n        }\n\n        parameter: {\n          registry: *\"index.docker.io\" | string\n          username: string\n          password: string\n        }\n\n        errs: [\n          if parameter.username == \"\" {\n            \"username must not be empty\"\n          },\n          if parameter.password == \"\" {\n            \"password must not be empty\"\n          },\n        ]\n```\n\nUnlike the legacy config-template format, the CUE follows the normal\nComponentDefinition structure. `output`, `outputs`, `parameter`, and `errs`\nare at the root; there is no outer `template: { ... }` block inside the CUE.\n\n### Config with inline properties\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: config.oam.dev/v1alpha1\nkind: Config\nmetadata:\n  name: team-a-registry\n  namespace: team-a\nspec:\n  definitionRef:\n    name: image-registry-config\n    namespace: vela-system\n  properties:\n    registry: index.docker.io\n    username: example-user\n    password: example-token\n  alias: Team A registry\n  description: Registry credentials used by Team A workloads\n```\n\nInline properties are useful for non-sensitive examples. They should not hold\nreal credentials because Config resources are not protected like Secrets.\n\n### Config with sensitive properties\n\nReal credentials should be stored in a Secret:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  name: team-a-registry-properties\n  namespace: team-a\ntype: Opaque\nstringData:\n  properties: |\n    {\n      \"registry\": \"index.docker.io\",\n      \"username\": \"team-a\",\n      \"password\": \"replace-me\"\n    }\n---\napiVersion: config.oam.dev/v1alpha1\nkind: Config\nmetadata:\n  name: team-a-registry\n  namespace: team-a\nspec:\n  definitionRef:\n    name: image-registry-config\n    namespace: vela-system\n  propertiesFrom:\n    secretRef:\n      name: team-a-registry-properties\n      key: properties\n```\n\n`properties` and `propertiesFrom` are mutually exclusive. The referenced\nproperties Secret must be in the same namespace as the Config.\n\n### Definition revisions\n\nAn optional revision makes rendering reproducible:\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  definitionRef:\n    name: image-registry-config\n    namespace: vela-system\n    revision: image-registry-config-v2\n```\n\nWhen no revision is supplied, the Config controller uses the current definition\nand re-renders the Config when that definition changes.\n\n### Config status\n\n```yaml\nstatus:\n  observedGeneration: 2\n  phase: Available\n  definition:\n    name: image-registry-config\n    namespace: vela-system\n    revision: image-registry-config-v3\n  secretRef:\n    name: team-a-registry\n  resources:\n    - apiVersion: v1\n      kind: Secret\n      name: team-a-registry\n      namespace: team-a\n    - apiVersion: v1\n      kind: ConfigMap\n      name: team-a-registry-info\n      namespace: team-a\n  conditions:\n    - type: Ready\n      status: \"True\"\n      reason: ReconcileSuccess\n```\n\nThe resolved definition revision in status records exactly which template\nproduced the current resources.\n\n## Reconciliation\n\nFor each Config, the controller performs the following sequence:\n\n1. Resolve `definitionRef`, including its namespace and optional revision.\n2. Verify that the definition is labeled as a config template.\n3. Verify that its declared primary workload is a `v1/Secret`.\n4. Load properties from `spec.properties` or `spec.propertiesFrom`.\n5. Render the definition with the shared ComponentDefinition CUE engine.\n6. Validate that the primary output is a Secret.\n7. Apply protected config metadata to the Secret.\n8. Store input properties when allowed by the sensitive-data contract.\n9. Apply the Secret and all auxiliary outputs.\n10. Remove resources that were present in the previous render but not the new\n    render.\n11. Record the resolved revision and resource inventory in status.\n\nThe controller watches Config resources, referenced ComponentDefinitions,\nreferenced DefinitionRevisions, input-property Secrets, and owned rendered\nresources. A definition or input change requeues affected Config resources.\n\n### Ownership and deletion\n\nThe primary Secret and same-namespace namespaced outputs can use controller\nowner references. Owner references cannot cover cross-namespace or\ncluster-scoped outputs. Therefore, Config needs a finalizer and an explicit\nresource inventory for complete cleanup.\n\nDeletion proceeds as follows:\n\n1. Delete every tracked auxiliary resource.\n2. Delete resources that cannot be garbage-collected through owner references.\n3. Allow Kubernetes garbage collection to remove owned resources.\n4. Remove the Config finalizer.\n\nThe controller must not delete an object it no longer owns. Apply metadata must\ninclude a stable Config UID so cleanup can verify ownership before deletion.\n\n### Updates and conflicts\n\nChanging `definitionRef.name` or the primary Secret type after the first\nsuccessful render should be rejected, matching the existing config behavior.\nProperty changes and unpinned definition updates re-render the resources.\n\nServer-side apply should use a dedicated field manager. A conflict on a field\nowned by another manager must surface as a Ready=False condition rather than\nsilently overwriting the field.\n\n## Security\n\n- Definitions marked sensitive must require `propertiesFrom` for declarative\n  Config resources. This prevents credentials from being stored in Config\n  specs, audit events, or ordinary API reads.\n- Sensitive input properties must not be copied into Config status.\n- Config read operations must continue to reject sensitive configs.\n- Protected labels and annotations are set by the controller after rendering,\n  so templates cannot override their values.\n- Definition lookup and output application must respect Kubernetes RBAC.\n- Templates must not receive an Application context implicitly. Only the\n  documented Config context is stable.\n\n## CLI compatibility\n\nThe existing commands can remain as a compatibility facade:\n\n- `vela config-template apply` translates legacy config-template CUE into a\n  marked ComponentDefinition.\n- `vela config-template list` lists ComponentDefinitions with\n  `config.oam.dev/template=true`.\n- `vela config-template show` reads the ComponentDefinition parameter schema.\n- `vela config-template delete` deletes the marked ComponentDefinition.\n- `vela config create` creates or updates a Config CR.\n- `vela config list`, `delete`, and `distribute` keep their existing user-facing\n  behavior.\n\nDuring migration, template loading may fall back to legacy\n`config-template-<name>` ConfigMaps. New writes should use ComponentDefinition.\nThe fallback can be removed in a later compatibility-breaking release.\n\nDistribution remains Application-backed because topology policies and resource\ntracking already solve the multi-cluster delivery problem. The Config controller\ndoes not need to duplicate that machinery.\n\n## Expanded writers\n\nExpanded writers are not part of ComponentDefinition rendering. Preserving this\nfeature requires one of these explicit choices:\n\n1. Store expanded-writer metadata in ComponentDefinition annotations or\n   `spec.extension` and execute it from the Config controller.\n2. Deprecate expanded writers for Config CRs while retaining them only for the\n   legacy imperative path.\n\nFor full backward compatibility, this design selects the first option. The\nexpanded-writer configuration belongs under `spec.extension.config` so it is\nversioned with the definition instead of spread across annotations.\n\n## Proposed code organization\n\n```text\napis/config.oam.dev/v1alpha1/\n└── config_types.go\n\ncharts/vela-core/crds/\n└── config.oam.dev_configs.yaml\n\npkg/config/\n├── component_definition.go\n├── renderer.go\n├── metadata.go\n└── compatibility.go\n\npkg/controller/config.oam.dev/v1alpha1/config/\n├── config_controller.go\n├── resource_apply.go\n├── resource_cleanup.go\n└── config_controller_test.go\n\npkg/webhook/config.oam.dev/v1alpha1/config/\n├── validating_handler.go\n└── validating_handler_test.go\n\nreferences/cli/\n├── config.go\n├── config_crd.go\n└── config_definition.go\n```\n\nThe following ConfigTemplate-specific files become unnecessary after migration:\n\n```text\napis/config.oam.dev/v1alpha1/configtemplate_types.go\ncharts/vela-core/crds/config.oam.dev_configtemplates.yaml\npkg/controller/config.oam.dev/v1alpha1/configtemplate/\npkg/webhook/config.oam.dev/v1alpha1/configtemplate/\n```\n\n## Testing\n\nUnit and integration tests should cover:\n\n- accepting a correctly labeled Secret ComponentDefinition\n- rejecting an unmarked ComponentDefinition\n- rejecting a non-Secret primary workload\n- rendering inline and Secret-backed properties\n- rejecting both property sources together\n- rendering and updating auxiliary outputs\n- deleting stale and finalizer-managed outputs\n- re-rendering after an unpinned definition update\n- retaining a pinned definition revision\n- refusing to expose sensitive properties\n- preserving protected labels and annotations\n- reporting CUE validation and apply conflicts in status\n- falling back to legacy template ConfigMaps during migration\n- preserving expanded-writer behavior\n- distributing the resulting Secret through the existing Application path\n\n## Migration sequence\n\n1. Add ComponentDefinition-backed loading to the Config controller and CLI.\n2. Teach `vela config-template apply` to create marked ComponentDefinitions.\n3. Retain read fallback for ConfigTemplate CRs and legacy ConfigMaps during the\n   compatibility window.\n4. Migrate built-in and addon config templates to ComponentDefinitions.\n5. Remove the ConfigTemplate controller and stop installing its CRD.\n6. Remove legacy read fallbacks in a later breaking release.\n\n## Consequences\n\n### Benefits\n\n- One definition API and one definition registration controller\n- Reuse of schema generation and definition revisions\n- Less duplicated CUE compilation code\n- Config templates become compatible with existing definition tooling\n- A focused Config controller retains config-specific security and lifecycle\n\n### Costs\n\n- Config still requires a controller; ComponentDefinition alone cannot\n  materialize Config resources.\n- The Config controller needs an adapter around the shared component renderer.\n- Config-specific metadata must be represented through labels, annotations, or\n  `spec.extension`.\n- Arbitrary outputs require finalizers and explicit resource tracking.\n- Legacy CUE templates need translation from the nested config-template shape\n  to the ComponentDefinition shape.\n\n## Final recommendation\n\nRemove the dedicated ConfigTemplate CRD and controller. Register config\ntemplates as marked ComponentDefinitions and retain a single Config controller\nthat renders them under the established Secret-oriented config contract. Reuse\nthe ComponentDefinition schema and revision machinery, but do not turn Config\ninto an alias for Application.\n
===================================================================
